1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,200
[Night. An enormous stadium, every floodlight blazing, sixty thousand seats, all of them empty. One small figure in a cream linen suit stands alone in the centre circle.]

2
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:04,415
NARRATOR: Sixty thousand seats. Every light switched on.

3
00:00:04,765 --> 00:00:07,162
NARRATOR: And only one ticket sold. Yours.

4
00:00:08,112 --> 00:00:11,262
[The stage at the far end explodes into light: a band in silhouette, a wall of beams, fireworks blooming over the empty stands. You throw your head back and laugh. Marcus, your huge head of security, stands two steps behind you.]

5
00:00:08,362 --> 00:00:12,551
NARRATOR: The biggest band on the planet. A private concert. For one person.

6
00:00:12,901 --> 00:00:14,401
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Is all of this... for me?

7
00:00:14,751 --> 00:00:16,103
MARCUS: All of it, Sir.

8
00:00:17,853 --> 00:00:21,003
[FREEZE: you mid-laugh, arms wide, fireworks frozen in the sky. The colour drains to gold and ink.]

9
00:00:18,103 --> 00:00:21,779
NARRATOR: This is your day. Today, you are the richest person on Earth.

10
00:00:22,129 --> 00:00:24,398
NARRATOR: And at midnight... you have to give it back.

11
00:00:26,348 --> 00:00:29,498
[The show's emblem: a golden sun with a single clock hand sweeping around it until the sun becomes a crescent moon.]

12
00:00:26,598 --> 00:00:29,571
NARRATOR: What if you were the richest person on Earth... for one day?

13
00:00:31,521 --> 00:00:34,671
[Dawn. You wake in a bed the size of a bus, on sheets like poured cream. Without anyone touching them, the curtains glide apart on a skyline of glass towers turning pink.]

14
00:00:32,671 --> 00:00:36,433
NARRATOR: Six a.m. You wake up in a bed bigger than most people's homes.

15
00:00:36,783 --> 00:00:40,203
NARRATOR: You haven't touched anything. The curtains open by themselves.

16
00:00:41,153 --> 00:00:44,303
[Marcus fills the doorway, earpiece glinting. You sit up, hair everywhere.]

17
00:00:41,403 --> 00:00:46,189
MARCUS: Good morning, Sir. Breakfast is ready. So are eleven people who need you.

18
00:00:46,539 --> 00:00:48,381
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Eleven? It's six o'clock.

19
00:00:48,731 --> 00:00:49,912
MARCUS: Twelve now.

20
00:00:50,462 --> 00:00:53,612
[FLASH: a small room in Lagos. A ceiling fan clicks round. A phone alarm buzzes on a thin mattress. A hand slaps it silent. We do not see whose.]

21
00:00:50,712 --> 00:00:55,071
NARRATOR: And somewhere else, in a small room in Lagos... the real one just woke up.

22
00:00:55,421 --> 00:00:57,903
NARRATOR: In your bed. Under your fan. To your alarm.

23
00:00:58,253 --> 00:01:00,394
NARRATOR: Remember that. We're coming back for it.

24
00:01:01,344 --> 00:01:04,494
[A marble bathroom the size of a tennis court. You stand at a gold sink, brushing your teeth, half asleep.]

25
00:01:01,594 --> 00:01:04,161
NARRATOR: You brush your teeth. Two minutes, like always.

26
00:01:04,511 --> 00:01:06,716
NARRATOR: But your money doesn't wait while you brush.

27
00:01:07,266 --> 00:01:10,416
[The tap coughs, and instead of water, gold coins pour out: a trickle, then a stream, piling in the sink.]

28
00:01:07,516 --> 00:01:10,531
NARRATOR: Your fortune is about eight hundred billion dollars.

29
00:01:10,881 --> 00:01:17,651
NARRATOR: Even growing slowly, five percent a year, it makes about seventy-six thousand dollars. Every single minute.

30
00:01:19,601 --> 00:01:22,751
[Coins overflow the sink and cascade over the edge, piling around your slippers. You stop brushing, foam on your lip, and stare down.]

31
00:01:20,151 --> 00:01:24,233
NARRATOR: By the time you spit, you've made more than two teachers earn in a whole year.

32
00:01:24,583 --> 00:01:26,703
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): ...I should brush my teeth more often.

33
00:01:27,653 --> 00:01:30,803
[A walk-in wardrobe as long as a corridor. Forty identical cream linen suits, perfectly spaced. You reach for the first one without looking.]

34
00:01:27,903 --> 00:01:31,259
NARRATOR: Your wardrobe. Forty suits. All exactly the same.

35
00:01:31,609 --> 00:01:36,822
NARRATOR: Because choosing what to wear is one decision too many, when you have a thousand more waiting.

36
00:01:37,372 --> 00:01:40,522
[A long white table by a window full of sky. Ada, your chief of staff, stands with a tablet.]

37
00:01:37,622 --> 00:01:40,552
ADA: Good morning, Sir. The chef would like to know what you want.

38
00:01:40,902 --> 00:01:41,933
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Anything?

39
00:01:42,283 --> 00:01:43,400
ADA: Any country.

40
00:01:43,950 --> 00:01:47,100
[As you name each dish, the table slides longer and a plate glides in: jollof rice, a basket of croissants, a sushi board, a tower of pancakes.]

41
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:48,005
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Jollof rice. French croissants. Japanese... everything.

42
00:01:48,355 --> 00:01:49,621
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): And pancakes.

43
00:01:49,971 --> 00:01:53,669
NARRATOR: Nobody says no to you. Nobody has said no to you in years.

44
00:01:54,219 --> 00:01:57,369
[A private airfield at morning. A white and gold jet lifts off the runway and banks into the sun.]

45
00:01:55,769 --> 00:01:57,013
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): What's for lunch?

46
00:01:57,363 --> 00:02:00,101
ADA: Wherever you like, Sir. Paris is two hours away.

47
00:02:00,451 --> 00:02:02,101
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Then... lunch in Paris.

48
00:02:03,651 --> 00:02:06,801
[Inside: cream leather, a sofa, clouds sliding past round windows. You kick off your shoes and wiggle your toes at the sky.]

49
00:02:03,901 --> 00:02:07,258
NARRATOR: No queue. No security line. No waiting at the gate.

50
00:02:07,608 --> 00:02:10,623
NARRATOR: The plane leaves when you sit down. Because it's yours.

51
00:02:11,973 --> 00:02:15,123
[Through the round window, far below: two green islands in a turquoise sea. Ada leans over and points.]

52
00:02:12,723 --> 00:02:14,672
ADA: That island down there is yours, Sir.

53
00:02:15,022 --> 00:02:16,032
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Which one?

54
00:02:16,382 --> 00:02:18,651
ADA: The one on the left. And the one on the right.

55
00:02:19,801 --> 00:02:22,951
[A sunny terrace, the iron tower on the skyline. You take a bite of warm bread and close your eyes.]

56
00:02:20,051 --> 00:02:22,618
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): This is the best bread I have ever eaten.

57
00:02:22,968 --> 00:02:24,767
ADA: Would you like to buy the bakery?

58
00:02:25,117 --> 00:02:26,106
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): ...Can I?

59
00:02:26,456 --> 00:02:27,295
ADA: Done.

60
00:02:27,845 --> 00:02:30,995
[Ada taps her watch. You look down at your plate. It is still full.]

61
00:02:28,095 --> 00:02:30,193
ADA: Sir, we have to leave. London at three.

62
00:02:30,543 --> 00:02:32,023
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): I've had one bite.

63
00:02:32,373 --> 00:02:34,001
ADA: It was a very good bite.

64
00:02:34,351 --> 00:02:36,599
NARRATOR: You flew to Paris. For eleven minutes.

65
00:02:37,149 --> 00:02:40,299
[Back in the cabin, over the sea. A phone buzzes on the table, glowing gold. Ada looks at it, then at you.]

66
00:02:37,399 --> 00:02:38,942
ADA: Sir... it's the President.

67
00:02:39,292 --> 00:02:40,238
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Of what?

68
00:02:40,588 --> 00:02:41,726
ADA: Does it matter?

69
00:02:42,276 --> 00:02:45,426
[You press the phone to your ear and look out at the clouds as a warm, important voice fills the cabin.]

70
00:02:42,526 --> 00:02:47,867
THE PRESIDENT (PHONE): My dear friend! I hope I'm not disturbing you. I have a small favour to ask.

71
00:02:48,217 --> 00:02:52,235
THE PRESIDENT (PHONE): A very small favour... with a very large number attached.

72
00:02:52,785 --> 00:02:55,935
[You lower the phone slowly. Ada is already holding out another one.]

73
00:02:53,035 --> 00:02:55,453
NARRATOR: A president. Calling you. For a favour.

74
00:02:55,803 --> 00:03:00,546
NARRATOR: And that's when you start to notice. Everybody who calls you today... wants something.

75
00:03:01,096 --> 00:03:04,246
[Noon, back in the city. Three identical black cars glide down an avenue of glass towers. You are in the middle one.]

76
00:03:02,046 --> 00:03:07,387
NARRATOR: Back in the city, you always ride in the middle car. There are always two more. Just in case.

77
00:03:07,937 --> 00:03:11,087
[Through the tinted window: a street-food cart on the corner, steam curling, office workers in a queue, laughing with the cook.]

78
00:03:08,187 --> 00:03:13,229
NARRATOR: Then you smell it. A food cart on the corner. Steam, and people laughing in the queue.

79
00:03:13,579 --> 00:03:15,975
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Stop the car. I want one of those.

80
00:03:16,525 --> 00:03:19,675
[You step out and walk toward the cart, smiling. Two guards instantly fall into step beside you.]

81
00:03:16,775 --> 00:03:19,556
NARRATOR: You haven't eaten from a street cart in twenty years.

82
00:03:19,906 --> 00:03:22,537
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Just me, Marcus. It's two minutes away.

83
00:03:23,087 --> 00:03:26,237
[Marcus steps into your path like a wall. Behind him: four guards, two cars, and a drone humming overhead.]

84
00:03:23,337 --> 00:03:24,945
MARCUS: Not today, Sir.

85
00:03:25,295 --> 00:03:27,073
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Marcus. It's a sandwich.

86
00:03:27,423 --> 00:03:30,993
MARCUS: Last time it was a sandwich. Then it was a crowd.

87
00:03:31,543 --> 00:03:34,693
[High and wide: a bubble of guards and cars around you in the middle of the avenue. The food cart, far away. The queue has turned to stare.]

88
00:03:32,293 --> 00:03:41,793
NARRATOR: Keeping one person this safe costs a fortune. One tech company spent about twenty-seven million dollars, in a single year, just protecting its boss.

89
00:03:42,143 --> 00:03:45,500
NARRATOR: You can buy anything on this street. Except a walk down it.

90
00:03:46,050 --> 00:03:49,200
[Back in the car. The food cart shrinks away in the rear window.]

91
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:50,254
NARRATOR: So you get back in the car. And you tell yourself that was the hard part.

92
00:03:50,604 --> 00:03:53,193
NARRATOR: It wasn't. The hard part hasn't happened yet.

93
00:03:54,343 --> 00:03:57,493
[A glass office at the top of the tower. A wall-sized calendar fills itself with coloured blocks, one after another, clicking into place until there is no space left.]

94
00:03:54,593 --> 00:03:57,843
NARRATOR: Your afternoon arrives in blocks. Fifteen minutes each.

95
00:03:58,193 --> 00:04:04,600
NARRATOR: Bosses of giant companies work around sixty-two hours a week. Nearly three-quarters of it... in meetings.

96
00:04:05,950 --> 00:04:09,100
[You at a huge desk, Ada at your shoulder, screens glowing. Outside, the sun races across the sky.]

97
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:08,981
ADA: Four hundred jobs in Brazil. Yes or no?

98
00:04:09,331 --> 00:04:10,085
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Yes.

99
00:04:10,435 --> 00:04:11,658
ADA: The new factory?

100
00:04:12,008 --> 00:04:12,762
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Yes.

101
00:04:13,112 --> 00:04:14,037
ADA: Your lunch?

102
00:04:14,387 --> 00:04:15,290
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): ...Later.

103
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:18,990
[On the big screen: a night map of a faraway town, every window lit. Ada asks one more question. You hesitate, say no, and a whole neighbourhood of windows goes dark at once.]

104
00:04:16,090 --> 00:04:18,828
ADA: And the old factory in Ohio. Keep it open?

105
00:04:19,178 --> 00:04:20,017
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): ...No.

106
00:04:20,367 --> 00:04:26,711
NARRATOR: One word. Somewhere, four hundred kitchens go quiet. And you decided it between two meetings, hungry.

107
00:04:27,861 --> 00:04:31,011
[Close on a sandwich on the desk with one bite missing. A hand in a white glove clears it away, untouched.]

108
00:04:28,111 --> 00:04:32,065
NARRATOR: Every yes changes somebody's life. And lunch... never happens.

109
00:04:33,215 --> 00:04:36,365
[Your phone lights up with a video call: a round, bearded face in a faded yellow T-shirt. You actually know him. You smile, for real.]

110
00:04:33,465 --> 00:04:37,461
NARRATOR: Then your phone rings. Not a president. Somebody you actually know.

111
00:04:37,811 --> 00:04:40,208
OLD FRIEND: Ehh! Look at you! Remember me? Chemistry class?

112
00:04:40,558 --> 00:04:42,656
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): You set the teacher's wig on fire!

113
00:04:43,206 --> 00:04:46,356
[You both laugh until you are wiping your eyes.]

114
00:04:43,456 --> 00:04:44,573
OLD FRIEND: It was an accident!

115
00:04:44,923 --> 00:04:46,658
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): It was a WIG!

116
00:04:47,008 --> 00:04:49,490
NARRATOR: It's the first time you've really laughed all day.

117
00:04:50,040 --> 00:04:53,190
[His smile shifts. He rubs the back of his neck. Your smile stays on your face, but it stops reaching your eyes.]

118
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:55,225
OLD FRIEND: So... listen. About my business. I just need a small push. You won't even notice it.

119
00:04:57,975 --> 00:05:01,125
[A slow montage of smiling faces reaching toward you: Ada with a document, a man at a party, a woman with a microphone, the old friend. In every open palm, a tiny gold glint.]

120
00:04:58,225 --> 00:05:02,179
NARRATOR: After that, you can't stop seeing it. Every smile comes with a hand.

121
00:05:02,529 --> 00:05:06,590
NARRATOR: And you start to wonder which of them would still call... if you had nothing at all.

122
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:10,890
[Evening. A glittering party under chandeliers. You raise a glass and tell the worst joke you know. Two hundred people in gowns and tuxedos burst out laughing.]

123
00:05:07,990 --> 00:05:09,960
NARRATOR: Evening. A party in your honour.

124
00:05:10,310 --> 00:05:14,989
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Why did the banker break up with the calculator? ...It just didn't count on him.

125
00:05:15,339 --> 00:05:20,381
NARRATOR: It's the worst joke you know. Two hundred people laugh like it's the best thing they've ever heard.

126
00:05:20,931 --> 00:05:24,081
[You try a test. You start a joke and simply stop in the middle. A beat of silence. Then the whole room roars even louder.]

127
00:05:21,181 --> 00:05:23,599
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): So a man walks into a bank, and...

128
00:05:23,949 --> 00:05:27,135
NARRATOR: So you try a joke with no ending. They laugh harder.

129
00:05:28,485 --> 00:05:31,635
[Night. The stadium again. The band at full power, light beams sweeping the empty stands, fireworks bursting over the roof.]

130
00:05:30,035 --> 00:05:34,245
NARRATOR: And then it's night, and the moment from the start of this story finally happens.

131
00:05:34,595 --> 00:05:39,061
NARRATOR: Every light. Every firework. The whole world's favourite song. Just for you.

132
00:05:41,211 --> 00:05:43,982
[Close: grinning, eyes full of fireworks, you turn to the seat beside you to share it...]

133
00:05:41,461 --> 00:05:43,282
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Did you see that? Did you—

134
00:05:44,132 --> 00:05:47,282
[The seat beside you is empty. So is the next one, and the next. Marcus stands three rows down, facing away, scanning the dark. The music sounds very far away.]

135
00:05:44,382 --> 00:05:47,419
NARRATOR: Sixty thousand seats. And nobody to turn to.

136
00:05:50,369 --> 00:05:53,519
[After the concert. A kitchen as big as a restaurant, all marble and steel, lit by one fridge light. You stand at the counter in your suit, eating cereal out of the box.]

137
00:05:50,619 --> 00:05:54,871
NARRATOR: You go home. Forty rooms. Three chefs, asleep. One fridge light.

138
00:05:55,221 --> 00:05:59,730
NARRATOR: The richest person on Earth eats dinner standing up. Out of a cereal box.

139
00:06:01,480 --> 00:06:04,630
[Wide: the huge dark apartment, the glittering city beyond the glass, and you, very small, in the one lit corner.]

140
00:06:01,730 --> 00:06:03,828
NARRATOR: It is very, very quiet up here.

141
00:06:06,178 --> 00:06:09,328
[A great gold clock face fills the screen, its hands sweeping toward the top.]

142
00:06:06,928 --> 00:06:08,919
NARRATOR: And then the clock does what clocks do.

143
00:06:10,869 --> 00:06:14,019
[At the stroke of twelve a white wind peels the stadium, the suit, the whole day away like paper, until there is only light.]

144
00:06:11,119 --> 00:06:12,727
NARRATOR: Midnight. You give it back.

145
00:06:13,077 --> 00:06:16,113
NARRATOR: But the real question isn't what you did with his day...

146
00:06:16,463 --> 00:06:18,028
NARRATOR: ...it's what he did with yours.

147
00:06:19,178 --> 00:06:22,328
[Morning in Lagos. You wake on your thin mattress under the clicking fan, in your grey hoodie. A voice outside the door could strip paint.]

148
00:06:19,928 --> 00:06:22,751
MAMA: Are you going to sleep until Christmas? Get up!

149
00:06:23,101 --> 00:06:25,605
NARRATOR: You're home. Your room. Your fan. Your mother.

150
00:06:26,155 --> 00:06:29,305
[Mama stands in the doorway with a wooden spoon, studying you like you are a stranger.]

151
00:06:26,405 --> 00:06:28,481
MAMA: And what happened to you yesterday?

152
00:06:28,831 --> 00:06:30,183
YOU: Yesterday?

153
00:06:30,533 --> 00:06:33,015
MAMA: Oh, you don't remember? Let me remind you.

154
00:06:33,565 --> 00:06:36,715
[Yesterday, in warm golden light: the market. You, but standing like a king, hand on heart, at Iya's orange stall.]

155
00:06:33,815 --> 00:06:37,065
NARRATOR: Yesterday, the richest person on Earth went to your market.

156
00:06:37,415 --> 00:06:39,513
IYA OSUN: Five oranges. Two thousand naira.

157
00:06:39,863 --> 00:06:42,622
YOU: Wonderful! I will give you four thousand.

158
00:06:42,972 --> 00:06:44,558
IYA OSUN: Ehn? Is that how haggling works?

159
00:06:45,108 --> 00:06:48,258
[Iya, stunned, piles three extra oranges into the bag. He bows to her, hand on his heart.]

160
00:06:45,358 --> 00:06:47,371
YOU: Thank you, madam. Truly.

161
00:06:47,721 --> 00:06:51,376
NARRATOR: He paid double. And thanked her. She is still telling everybody.

162
00:06:51,926 --> 00:06:55,076
[A packed yellow danfo bus rattles down the street. Inside, squashed between strangers and one very calm chicken, he is grinning like he is on a theme-park ride.]

163
00:06:52,676 --> 00:06:56,651
NARRATOR: He took the bus. Squashed between twelve strangers and one chicken.

164
00:06:57,001 --> 00:07:01,787
NARRATOR: Nobody looked at him. Nobody wanted anything from him. He loved every minute.

165
00:07:03,337 --> 00:07:06,487
[Evening. The family sits on a floor mat around one big shared plate. Mama drops the biggest piece of meat on his side without a word.]

166
00:07:03,587 --> 00:07:05,195
MAMA: Eat. You're too thin.

167
00:07:05,545 --> 00:07:11,312
NARRATOR: At dinner, your mother gave him the biggest piece of meat. Without being asked. Without wanting anything back.

168
00:07:11,862 --> 00:07:15,012
[Close: he looks down at the meat, very still, eyes shining.]

169
00:07:12,112 --> 00:07:15,682
NARRATOR: The richest person on Earth didn't say anything for a long time.

170
00:07:17,832 --> 00:07:20,982
[Outside by the water drum, he washes the family's plates in a plastic basin, sleeves rolled up, humming. Mama watches from the doorway, deeply suspicious.]

171
00:07:18,082 --> 00:07:21,481
NARRATOR: Then he washed every plate. Nobody asked. He was humming.

172
00:07:21,831 --> 00:07:24,441
MAMA: Who are you, and what have you done with my child?

173
00:07:24,991 --> 00:07:28,141
[Cards on the mat. Your little sister slaps down a winning hand. He clutches his chest in fake agony.]

174
00:07:25,241 --> 00:07:26,443
LITTLE SISTER: I win! Again!

175
00:07:26,793 --> 00:07:28,571
NARRATOR: He let your sister win at cards.

176
00:07:28,921 --> 00:07:31,297
LITTLE SISTER: You let me win. I know you did. I still won.

177
00:07:31,847 --> 00:07:34,997
[Just before midnight. He stands at your window, looking out at the street: a generator humming, music from a radio, a neighbour laughing.]

178
00:07:32,097 --> 00:07:35,859
NARRATOR: And just before midnight, he stood at your window. For a long time.

179
00:07:36,209 --> 00:07:38,136
NARRATOR: Like someone who didn't want to leave.

180
00:07:40,686 --> 00:07:43,836
[Back to this morning. Mama, spoon raised, narrows her eyes at you.]

181
00:07:40,936 --> 00:07:45,082
MAMA: You were strange yesterday. Polite. You washed the plates without being told.

182
00:07:45,432 --> 00:07:46,677
MAMA: Do it again.

183
00:07:47,227 --> 00:07:50,377
[Alone on your mattress, you look around your small room: the fan, the cracked window, your sister's drawing taped to the wall. Sunlight on all of it.]

184
00:07:47,477 --> 00:07:50,257
NARRATOR: So. One day as the richest person on Earth.

185
00:07:50,607 --> 00:07:52,087
NARRATOR: Would you have given it back?

186
00:07:54,237 --> 00:07:57,387
[A packed stadium roars. Floodlights flare. A football sits alone on the penalty spot.]

187
00:07:55,187 --> 00:07:57,925
NARRATOR: Next time, you borrow somebody else's day.

188
00:07:58,275 --> 00:08:03,167
NARRATOR: Ninety thousand people. One penalty. And every one of them is screaming your name.

189
00:08:05,117 --> 00:08:08,267
[The emblem settles in the dark: a gold sun circled by one clock hand. Beside it, a glimpse of the next borrowed day.]

190
00:08:05,367 --> 00:08:08,489
NARRATOR: Tell us in the comments: whose day should you borrow next?
